WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Let's get started, folks, with another speedrun game.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
For those watching on YouTube, I am currently in Oslo, Norway, commentating the Chess Kit Cup.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
So apologies in advance for any audio or video problems, but hopefully things hold up.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
And we are playing Black against Anik0506.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
And as is our custom, we will attempt an Accelerated Dragon.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.000
And finally, we get an Open Sicilian, which has been rare in the speedrun thus far.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
We actually have had just a handful of Accelerated Dragons.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Let's see how it holds up against a strong player.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
And this is our first Marozzi bind. This is very exciting.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
We have not faced the Marozzi bind to date.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.000
At a lower level, it's much more common to play Knight to c3, which is sort of the other main move.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The Marozzi binds, which is characterized by the pawns on e4 and c4, this is a classic bind.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:03.000
And in general, in chess, a bind is a situation where two or more pawns control a set of key central squares.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
In this case, they really only control one square, the d5 square.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
But the danger of this positionally is that it is very, very hard for Black to break free in the center with d5.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
And so if Black handles this opening improperly, what often happens is that Black's position becomes incredibly cramped.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.000
So it's not like White is trying to checkmate us.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The Marozzi bind is dangerous from a positional standpoint.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Because handled improperly, you get a situation where Black just runs out of ideas

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
and is basically, you know, hanging around on the bottom two or three ranks.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
So the line that I recommend against the Marozzi is not the most popular, Bg7.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
It's actually to start with the move, Knight to f6.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Now, of course, the appeal of this move is that it hits the e4 pawn straight away.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
And I think our opponent already responds with a somewhat improper move, f3.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Instead, the common response here is Knight b1 to c3, which I'll address separately after the game.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
And I'll kind of give you the outline of the system that I recommend.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
So I think we can basically do the same thing against the move, f3.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
What you want to do here is actually take on d4.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
So you invite White's queen to recapture.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
And it may seem at first sight that this can only benefit White.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Why is that? Well, it's because the queen on d4 looks unassailable.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.000
But what you should realize, what you should realize...

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Oh, apparently I've lost to this guy.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Oh, it's the guy I disconnected against. That's funny.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.000
So there was a speedrun game for those watching on YouTube where I got auto-disconnected

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
and then it auto-resigned, and it was against this guy.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.000
So I have my chance at revenge.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.000
But what you should notice is that once the bishop is deployed to the long diagonal,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
the queen is going to be under very serious fire,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
and there's going to be threats of a discovered attack with this knight.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.000
But we don't want to go Bishop g7 immediately,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
because we don't want to give White the opportunity to play e5,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
because that'll force our knight back to g8,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
and why should we allow that kind of stuff, right?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Although, on the other hand, after Bishop g7, e5, knight g8, White is so poorly developed

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.000
that that e5 pawn is going to be very hard to hold.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
So I actually think that we can start with Bishop g7.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
This is a very tricky move.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
And my intuition tells me that after e5, knight g8, which is what happens on the board,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.000
White is going to be unable to hold onto his center without that center starting to collapse.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:39.000
So who can tell me what is the best way of attacking this e5 pawn using the pin against White's queen?

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.000
That's kind of the key element of this position, is that pin,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
which allows us to put pressure on White's center.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Of course, it's the obvious d6, which is what we should start with.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Bishop f4. Okay, I don't think this is the area I think our opponent is doing

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
what he did in our previous game, which is just playing instantly and, you know, kind of bluffing.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
So let's think here for a second.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Now, of course, we have the option of playing pawn takes pawn.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Then a queen trade is forced, but then White will castle queenside with check

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.000
and develop an initiative in the endgame.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.000
In that position, it looks like White's got pretty decent compensation for the pawn.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
So that's a backup option.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Now, of course, we have the option of developing the knight to h6 and trying to castle quickly,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.000
but that's a bit anticlimactic because our general strategy is to put pressure on this e5 pawn.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Yeah, there is this very interesting move some of you are suggesting, which is to play queen to a5,

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
and the idea is to not only pin the knight, but more to the point,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.000
is the fact that it attacks the e5 pawn. It threatens a fork.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:46.000
But after queen a5, White has the very annoying move, queen to d5.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.000
And that's a hard move for us to deal with because in the event of the queen trade,

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.000
White will be threatening knight c7.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.000
So after queen a5, queen d5, we can try to drop our queen back to b6.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Not a bad option, but again, White has this check on b5, forcing the queens off the board.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.000
And again, we're experiencing problems where the knight always threatens to get to the c7 square.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.000
So it's actually quite tricky to play this position.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.000
I'm also looking at some developing moves here.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
We don't actually have to make a move to attack this pawn.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.000
We can play a simple move like bishop e6.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.000
So I actually think that that is the most practical option.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Rather than trying to do everything in one move, we can just bring our freaking pieces out.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.000
So bishop e6 is possible.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.000
And then in the event that White castles queen side, then we can play pawn takes e5.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
And in contrast to the immediate pawn takes e5, notice that our rook is going to be protecting our queen.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.000
So this is the kind of move which I think a lot of lower-ranked players would find it very difficult to make

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:58.000
because we're not attacking e5. We're not doing what one might think we have to do.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
And instead, we're just kind of keeping the tension and making a regular developing move.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.000
And I actually can't believe our opponent is thinking.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.000
Clearly, I'm doing something right then.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.000
I'm also not saying that it's the best move. It might not be the best move.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Now, you might also wonder, well, wait a second.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Why are we not putting our bishop on the quote-unquote more aggressive f5 square?

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Now, bishop f5 does not prevent long castles because by rule, it's the king that cannot cross a defended square.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
The rook is allowed to cross a defended square.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.000
So in response to bishop f5, White could indeed castle queen side.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.000
What I like about bishop e6, though, is that it does pressure the c4 pawn, which could become important later.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.000
And the second thing it does is it controls the d5 square,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:46.000
which is a rather annoying jumping off point for White's knight in certain variations.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.000
So I think that we're in pretty good shape here.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.000
In retrospect, maybe it was not the best idea to allow e5.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.000
The classic treatment is to play d6 in order to prevent e5.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:03.000
So after knight c3, which is the main move, after takes takes, you're supposed to play d6 there.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.000
It's actually very important in that particular version to not allow e5.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.000
The difference here is that White didn't use this move to develop a piece.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:20.000
So I concluded that we can get away with allowing e5.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.000
So what are we threatening? Okay, queen e4.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.000
That's a good move. It's a great move.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.000
Now, we can start to play tactically here, and I have a very exciting idea.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.000
Now, we shouldn't fall asleep because what is the point of queen e4?

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Well, it defends b7, so some of you might be thinking,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.000
oh, well, let's defend the pawn with something like rook b8.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.000
Well, that would be a very, very bad move because White is now able to take on d6.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Why does that favor White? Because you might say, oh, but doesn't that open up our bishop?

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.000
Well, if you look more carefully after ed, ed, the cons outweigh the pros

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.000
because that d6 pawn becomes an incredibly vulnerable weakness,

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.000
and White can castle queenside there, and immediately the d6 pawn collapses,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.000
and our king is going to stay stranded in the center.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.000
So let's not touch that with a 10-foot pole, which gives us essentially one option.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.000
I don't like queen b6 here because that actually invites White to castle queenside, right?

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.000
It invites White to do what he wants to do anyway.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.000
So I actually think we have to take on e5 and resolve the tension in the center.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:29.000
That's going to help us develop our kingside more quickly.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.000
Bishop takes e5. Now, we've got two options.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.000
The first is the sort of mundane option, which equalizes.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.000
The second is the Naroditsky approach, and it's very much in the spirit of the accelerated dragon.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.000
It might be dubious subjectively, but I think we can make it work.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:50.000
So the simple option is to trade on e5 and then play knight g8 to f6,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.000
and then to castle kingside, and that position is, I think, approximately equal.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:02.000
The second option, let's see who can come up with it. What else is available to us in this position?

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.000
Actually, we have a third option, which might be even better.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.000
Yeah, a lot of you guys are finding it.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.000
It's not f5 because f5 is a very weakening move.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:16.000
That would be one movitis. The queen just drops back, and what have we achieved, right?

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.000
We've merely deprived this bishop of a very important defender.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:27.000
In the same spirit, the move that I missed initially was bishop to f5. Bishop to f5.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Oh, but white's got a sick tactic there. Oh my gosh.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:36.000
White can take on b7 there, and after bishop takes bishop,

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.000
drop the queen back to b5 and then win the bishop back on e5 with an extra pawn.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.000
Man, I am not getting lucky in this speedrun.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.000
Now, we can go for that line consciously because it accelerates our development,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:52.000
but in the same spirit, we can also simply play knight f6, queen takes b7 in castle kingside

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:59.000
and try to develop an advantage and then somehow punish white for keeping the king in the center.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:02.000
So let me think about which option is better.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Yeah, it feels like I did something wrong here.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:12.000
I have been getting very consistently outplayed in this speedrun in the last couple of games, it seems,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:18.000
but let's not panic. It might be necessary for us to play bishop takes e5, queen takes e5, and knight f6.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:25.000
And this is something that constitutes an important skill at a higher level as well,

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:30.000
which is to basically admit, yeah, it's not that I messed up, I don't think I messed up seriously,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.000
but I didn't want to go for this line initially because it's boring

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.000
and because giving away your fianchettoed bishop can be a problem.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
Now, it should be pointed out that if we compare the placement of the remaining bishops,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.000
our bishop is definitely better than its counterpart.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.000
And the reason I say that is because of this c4 pawn, which is really blunting this bishop on f1.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:55.000
On the other hand, white's got an active queen, white's got slightly better control of the center,

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:01.000
so this pawn can be considered both a strength and a weakness, which is very common at a higher level.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.000
And of course, also, this pawn on f3 is not, the pawn itself is not a weakness,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:12.000
of course, as I've pointed out many times, when there's a pawn like this on f3 or on f4,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.000
you always have this idea of playing queen to b6,

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.000
and in general, exploiting this diagonal when white casts a short h4.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.000
Our opponent kind of going for the kill here.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.000
I'm not scared of this move because white doesn't have any development here,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.000
so this just cannot work from a perspective of chess principles.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.000
But we should take this idea seriously.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.000
We've got, again, several options.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:39.000
We could play h5, which would be the move that we play if we respect our opponent's idea.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Alternatively, we could just castle in white's face and allow h5.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.000
It's not entirely a paper tiger.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.000
Like, this could get very dangerous for us, but I'm just very scared.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.000
I mean, it's hard to refute our opponent's play.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.000
We could then just play queen to d6 and trade queens and try to win the endgame.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.000
That would be an option.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Actually, I don't dislike that option.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.000
Let's start by castling, so let's start by completing our development.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.000
There's no way that h5 directly leads to checkmate.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.000
I called BS on that.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Okay, so what are our options here?

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.000
Well, we can play knight takes h5, and that's a hard move to evaluate.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.000
Definitely we don't get mated right away after knight takes h5.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:30.000
What worries me about that move is the long-term problems along the h file,

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.000
but I did just come up with a cool defensive idea.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.000
So what is white likely to do after knight takes h5?

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.000
Well, I think the obvious move there is g4,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.000
but after g4, we drop our knight back to f6,

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:47.000
and we ask, well, what has white really achieved from sacrificing that pawn?

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.000
The queen could then drop back to h2,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:54.000
but the h7 pawn would be very well protected by our knight,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.000
and that in general would not be a scary type of idea.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.000
I think that we should call BS on this and take this pawn fearlessly.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.000
I don't see a clear follow-up for white. I just don't see it.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:16.000
Maybe something exists, but I do not see it.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.000
Well, if he takes the knight, then we take back,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.000
and I don't see a follow-up there either.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.000
I don't think there's checkmate.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.000
Our opponent is probably going to opt for something like that

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.000
that would be consistent with what he's been doing,

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:37.000
but I'm kind of calling the bluff here.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.000
Yeah, so maybe there's some sort of hidden tactic.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.000
I don't rule that out at all.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Again, entirely possible that there's some perpetual there

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.000
or there's some line that leads to a strong attack,

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.000
but our opponent doesn't go for it, and this was my idea.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:57.000
This is the move that initially caused me to rule out knight takes h5,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.000
but I think there's a brilliant defensive move in this position.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.000
How do you find this defensive move?

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.000
Well, you should always have a wish list in mind,

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.000
and I've talked about this before.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
What are the items that you want to achieve

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.000
to further the causes of your position?

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.000
I think the top item on the wish list,

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.000
and this should be obvious to everybody, is a queen trade.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
If we can trade queens, then we're simply up a pawn in the endgame.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:22.000
The moment you tell yourself that this move should become

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:25.000
maybe not obvious, but you should at least be on your radar,

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.000
and that's queen d8 to b8,

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.000
which is not hard to see conceptually.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.000
There's different types of difficulty in chess.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.000
There's conceptual difficulty where a move might be easy to see,

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:40.000
but it might be hard to understand why it's good.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.000
This is the kind of move that when it's played,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.000
everybody says, ah, well, of course, queen b8,

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.000
but it can be hard to see geometrically

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.000
because you don't make this move often,

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.000
and a lot of you might have been tempted by a move like queen to b6,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:56.000
which I think would have been incredibly dangerous.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.000
The additional benefit of this move is that

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.000
if white declines the queen trade with queen to g5,

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.000
how should we proceed in that position?

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:07.000
This is the sort of second part of our idea,

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.000
insisting on your concept.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.000
Queen g3, exactly, queen g3.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Now, queen f4 would be a blunder there

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.000
because of rook takes h5,

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.000
grabbing the knight and protecting the queen at the same time,

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:23.000
a very, very common type of blunder.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:29.000
But in the same vein, queen g5, queen g3 check forces the queen,

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:33.000
and after our knight gets to g3, don't stop calculating there.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.000
Notice that white experiences problems with this pawn on c4

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:39.000
because we will be then threatening to take on f1

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.000
and take on c4 with two extra pawns.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.000
Now you might say, well, white can move rook h4 in that position.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.000
That's some high-level calculation,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.000
but you don't have to calculate any further

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.000
because we know for a fact that we are up a pawn

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.000
and we're chilling in that position.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.000
So if white plays rook h5 now,

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.000
then we will take back on h5.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:01.000
White has a check on g5,

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.000
but after king h8, I don't see a follow-up,

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:07.000
and it occurs to me now that white did have a draw in the previous position.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.000
White could have drawn the game here by force

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.000
with a very, very advanced tactical concept,

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.000
which I'll show you after the game,

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.000
but it did in fact start with rook takes h5.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:23.000
So at this point, I think we're very much in control of the game.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.000
And that is obviously a good sign.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.000
Now that doesn't mean we've won. We're just up a pawn.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
But we're up a pawn, and we still have the advantage

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.000
of our superior bishop.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:42.000
Our knight on h5 suddenly becomes an asset

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.000
rather than a deficiency

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.000
because it's threatening to jump onto one of these weak dark squares,

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.000
and it's just nicely played.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.000
I mean, it could come back to f6 at any time

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.000
and kind of rejoin society,

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:56.000
but it's doing well on this little island here.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.000
And our bishop is also doing a great job

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.000
because it's taking the sting out of the move knight to d5,

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
simply bishop takes d5.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.000
So again, to convert such endgames,

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.000
which is one of the hardest skills in chess,

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.000
when you should never feel bad when you fail to convert

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.000
an endgame like this, right, where you're, let's say, up a pawn,

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.000
because it's genuinely very, very difficult,

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:21.000
and white has some assets too.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.000
I mean, his pieces are active. He's got a pawn majority.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:26.000
It's just we've got an extra pawn on the other side of the board.

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:29.000
So this requires tremendous precision.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.000
I'm going to accelerate a little bit here

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:33.000
because we're kind of low on time.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.000
Broadly speaking, of course, we want to trade pieces, right?

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.000
That's a pretty straightforward statement.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.000
We want to trade pieces.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.000
If we can get all of the rooks off the board,

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.000
I think it'll be easier for us

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.000
to sort of convert our four-on-two

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.000
pawn majority on the king side.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.000
So there's a couple of ideas here.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:55.000
We can throw the knight onto g3.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:56.000
That's one option.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.000
But then white will play bishop d3.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:01.000
The knight could potentially be maneuvered back to f5,

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.000
but I'm not totally convinced

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.000
that the trade of bishop for knight is positive for us.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.000
I think that we should make a less committal move

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.000
and just play rook fd8.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.000
I think we should keep our trump cards in our pocket

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.000
and keep the knight on h5,

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.000
and we should get a pair of rooks off the board.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.000
Now the downside of this approach

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.000
is that we've given up the d-file.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:24.000
But if you look more carefully, this bishop is really a hero

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:27.000
because it's holding this penetration square on d7.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.000
Yeah, white has the d-file,

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.000
but I don't think that white has a lot that he can gain

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.000
from the control over the d-file.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.000
Now we can do a couple of different things.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:39.000
In general, our opponent's plan

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:42.000
is probably to start pushing his pawn majority

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.000
with something like c5.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:47.000
So a move that occurs to me as a kind of prophylactic measure

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:51.000
is to play b6 and try to halt the progress of the pawn.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.000
Alternatively, rook c8 comes to mind,

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:55.000
but that's a little bit passive.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.000
We can still play knight g3.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:00.000
Possibly white will play bishop d3 there,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.000
and maybe in that position

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.000
we can already consider knight f5.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.000
Although, again, I'm not totally sure

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.000
who benefits with that trade.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:11.000
I think it might be us, though.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.000
Yeah, I'm inclined to play knight g3.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:18.000
I think now's the time to reap the rewards of our knight,

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.000
and I think that we should play knight f5

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.000
and essentially ask white a question.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.000
Okay, our opponent is answering questions

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.000
very quickly in this game.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.000
We have a bishop versus knight position.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.000
What changed my mind is that we've traded a pair of rooks.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.000
I think with only one pair of rooks on the board,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.000
white's rook and knight look very impressive,

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.000
but again, all that white can really do here

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.000
is pose one-move threats,

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.000
and that's what our opponent is doing.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.000
Obviously, we drop our bishop back to e6.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:47.000
There's no other squares, and knight d5.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.000
Okay, so that asks us a big question.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.000
Do we want to trade bishop for knight?

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:54.000
And your answer should not just be yes uncritically

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:58.000
because after bishop takes knight, rook takes bishop,

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:01.000
what's the complicating factor in that rook endgame?

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:03.000
Well, it's the fact that white is infiltrating

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:07.000
the 7th rank by force, which is a big deal.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:10.000
But upon closer examination,

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.000
I think that we can actually keep the rook

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.000
away from the 7th rank.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.000
How? By playing what move?

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.000
King f8 there.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:21.000
And if white still drops the rook on the 7th rank,

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.000
what's our strategy there?

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:26.000
It's a defensive strategy.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:31.000
So bishop takes d5, rook takes d5, king f8, rook d7.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:35.000
We go king e8, rook c7, king d8,

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:38.000
and we drive the rook out of that 7th rank,

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.000
and that should already be winning.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.000
So I think we should take.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.000
Maybe our opponent will take back. No, okay.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.000
And we should go king f8.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:49.000
Now, more broadly, what we want to do is go king e8

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.000
and then trade the rooks.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.000
And pawn endgames are going to be winning most of them.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.000
Although if you've watched my pawn endgame videos,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.000
you will know that there is a complicating factor

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.000
on the king side, unfortunately.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.000
What is it? What can white do

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.000
to try to make it difficult for us

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.000
to exploit our pawn majority?

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:09.000
What move can white potentially make at some point?

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.000
Yeah, so white can do what's called the deep freeze,

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:13.000
and that's where one pawn

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:16.000
stops two or more pawns from advancing.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.000
And this is the most common type of deep freeze,

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:21.000
a very advanced move, g5, not easy to make,

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.000
because you have to understand why you're making it.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.000
You're making it to halt the progress of the h-pawn.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:29.000
h5 then, of course, meets with en passant.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:31.000
So even some of those pawn endgames,

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.000
and I have some examples,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:35.000
you could have a pawn endgame where you're up

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:37.000
to even two or even three pawns,

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.000
but because there's a deep freeze situation,

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:42.000
the pawn majority on the other flank decides the game.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.000
So we shouldn't just uncritically go for that rook endgame.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:47.000
Now, deep freezes can be fought, right?

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:51.000
This g5 pawn could be contested with a timely f6,

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:54.000
but then white could play f4.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.000
So we'll have to calculate pawn endgames concretely.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.000
So given the opportunity,

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:01.000
we might actually want to play h6,

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:03.000
so that if white ever plays g5,

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:05.000
well, we take the sting out of it,

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.000
and we can actually play that now,

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:09.000
but we could also go king e8.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.000
So what's more important?

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.000
Well, it's hard to say.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.000
That requires some calculation over everything,

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:18.000
as Canty says.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:23.000
Now, my temptation is to bring our king into the game.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:26.000
Yeah, I think we should do that.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:29.000
I think we should start with king e8.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.000
And I'm really not sure.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.000
Okay, now we can again play h6

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.000
and hold off the pawn majority.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:38.000
We could play e6 and chase the rook away.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:39.000
That's an interesting option.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.000
We could play rook d8,

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:42.000
but again, I'm not convinced that that pawn endgame

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:45.000
is winning because of this g5 idea,

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.000
which is quite annoying.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.000
So definitely we shouldn't hurry.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:49.000
I think we should play h6,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.000
but oh, but then we can go rook d1 and rook h1.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:53.000
Wow.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.000
That's nuts.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:59.000
So maybe f6 is a better way to do the same thing.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:04.000
Maybe f6 is an interesting approach here.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:06.000
Man, this is not easy.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.000
e6 is possible as well.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:14.000
Rook d1, king e7, rook h1, rook h5,

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:17.000
and white de-freezes us again.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:18.000
But then we play e5.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.000
No, I think that one's winning.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:24.000
Oh, so e6 is a very good-looking move.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:26.000
Yeah, I think we should go e6

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.000
and just get this rook off of d5.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.000
This also forces white to kind of decide

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.000
where he wants to keep the rook

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:34.000
because if he keeps it on the fifth rank,

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:37.000
then potentially he gives us the d-file.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:40.000
Well, asking questions like these is always a good idea.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:42.000
Now, I have a higher-level plan in mind.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.000
Here's the ideal scenario.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.000
Again, it's the sort of wishlist method

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.000
where you say, okay,

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:50.000
what do I want to achieve in the ideal scenario?

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:52.000
Well, ideally, we want one of two things.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.000
Either we want a pawn endgame

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:56.000
where we don't have a deep freeze situation,

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.000
so in some way we've prevented g5.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.000
That would be winning

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:00.000
because the four-on-two-pawn majority

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.000
will beat the three-on-two-pawn majority

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:04.000
on the other side.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.000
But the second wishlist item

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.000
is a situation where we create

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.000
a passed h-pawn

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:12.000
and get this b8 rook over to h8.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.000
That would basically end the game right away.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:16.000
Those types of situations are almost always winning

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:18.000
in rook endgames,

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.000
and I'll show you kind of a stock example

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:22.000
of that after the game.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.000
There's a famous classical game that really

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.000
shows the extent to which

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.000
that situation often decides

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.000
the game in a rook endgame.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.000
So, after rook to d1,

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:35.000
you would be forgiven for thinking

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:37.000
let's play h5, let's go, let's create the passer,

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:39.000
but if you calculate after g8,

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.000
g8, rook h1,

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.000
the rook can't jump over the king.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:45.000
That is why after rook d1

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.000
we have to go king e7.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:49.000
If, after that, white plays

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:51.000
rook h1 and hits the h7 pawn,

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.000
we are just in time to play

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:55.000
rook to h8, and what do we threaten

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:57.000
after we play rook h8?

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:59.000
And what do you think white should do

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.000
in response to that? Just testing your understanding.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:03.000
Okay, rook b5.

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.000
The move there was, of course, h5.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.000
Right, so once we play rook h8,

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.000
we're ready to play h5.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:11.000
Our opponent is taking a fundamentally different approach.

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:13.000
He is trying to hold off the move h5,

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:15.000
and I think this is the best move.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:17.000
Okay, so let's think.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.000
Again, we have options.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:21.000
I think here we should take the opportunity

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.000
to play h6.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.000
This is the time that we should play h6.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:27.000
The reason why is that if instead

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.000
you play a move like king to d7,

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.000
and maybe we should have played a6.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:33.000
Now that I think about it,

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.000
yeah, maybe a6 is better,

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.000
but it doesn't really matter.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.000
The thing is, white was threatening to play g5 there.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:43.000
Now I think we're in great shape,

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:45.000
but we have to be extremely precise.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.000
This guy is

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.000
wildly, wildly underrated.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:51.000
Let's see.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:53.000
I think that we should go king e7,

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.000
but I'm not sure.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:57.000
Well, we have to bring our king up

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.000
some way,

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:01.000
because if we give a check on d8,

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:03.000
we simply help white's king get closer

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.000
to our side of the territory.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:07.000
So the ultimate choice here is between

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:09.000
king d7 and king e7,

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:11.000
and my intuition tells me that we should

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:13.000
stay a little bit closer to our kingside pawns.

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:15.000
Now we're very, very close

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:17.000
to being able to create a passer,

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.000
either with h5,

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.000
and in order to be able to play h5,

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.000
we're going to have to kick this rook off the 5th rank,

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:25.000
so in our pocket, we have this move,

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:27.000
a6, which we can throw in

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:29.000
essentially when we're ready to play h5,

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:31.000
which is now.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.000
But white is very active here,

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.000
so there are some complicating factors.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.000
The king is super active.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.000
Of course, the rook is pressuring b7,

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:41.000
and we want to be careful about playing b6.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.000
I mean, maybe that was the best move,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.000
and actually that was the best move, wasn't it?

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.000
Should have played b6, simply.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:49.000
For some reason, I thought c5 was a problem,

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.000
but it's not, because bc is a check.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.000
It's late.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:55.000
I'm missing some details in this endgame,

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:57.000
but I still think we're winning.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.000
Yeah, but our opponent,

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:01.000
this guy is

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:03.000
incredibly sharp.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:05.000
Incredibly sharp.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.000
He punishes us for not playing b6.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:09.000
But it's not

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:11.000
the end of the world.

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:13.000
Far from the end of the world.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:15.000
We have a choice here,

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.000
because c6 is not a serious threat, actually.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.000
c6 is not a threat at all.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.000
So we have options.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.000
We can play f6,

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.000
which is an ugly looking move.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.000
But the benefit of f6,

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:29.000
why would we do that?

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:31.000
Well, it would be to prevent white's king

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:33.000
from infiltrating e5.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:35.000
We can also play Rd8, Rd7,

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:37.000
and essentially shift the positioning of our rook,

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.000
so that in addition to defending b7,

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.000
it is also doing something productive

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:43.000
and keeping white's king away.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:45.000
I think there are multiple ways to win here.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.000
I really like the look of Rd8 and Rd7.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.000
I also like the look of h5.

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:53.000
Basically not procrastinating.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:55.000
But then if our rook gets stuck here,

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:57.000
that might be an issue.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:59.000
Because eventually we'll need to go

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.000
Kd7, Kc6 to get that rook behind the pawn.

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:03.000
So man, this is really hard.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:13.000
Okay, let's go Rd8 check.

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:15.000
This is what my intuition tells me to do.

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:19.000
h5 might be committing a little too early,

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:21.000
I agree.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.000
But these are...

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:25.000
Hopefully if you're newer to chess,

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:27.000
you're beginning to see through the lens of this endgame

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:29.000
why rook endgames are considered

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.000
so complex and hard to learn.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.000
It's because in the middlegame,

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.000
the position, you have these things that you can hold onto

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:37.000
to make sense of the position.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:39.000
In rook endgames,

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.000
there's always 5 or 7 candidate moves

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:43.000
and all of them look reasonable

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:45.000
and all of them adhere to different plans.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:47.000
Like, do you start by creating a passer?

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:49.000
Do you improve the position of the rook?

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:51.000
Do you improve your pawn structure on the kingside?

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:53.000
There's so many things that you can do

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.000
and all of them seem approximately equivalent.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.000
And very often there's only one winning path.

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.000
So you're essentially relying

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:01.000
in most cases purely on your intuition

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.000
and on a little bit of calculation.

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:05.000
And most of the calculation

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:07.000
that you do in rook endgames

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.000
involves figuring out who wins

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.000
so a pawn endgame, that's called liquidation

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.000
and it's sometimes calculating

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.000
pawn races and concrete things

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.000
like that.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:19.000
So, rook endgames are incredibly hard

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.000
almost nobody can play

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:23.000
them without mistakes.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:25.000
Magnus, definitely the best rook endgame

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.000
player of all time. Capablanca was well known

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:29.000
for that as well.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:31.000
So it's very very hard to play without blundering.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:33.000
I'll give you some recommendations

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.000
on endgames after the game,

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:37.000
but here I have to focus

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:39.000
because two and a half minutes

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:41.000
is not a lot of time.

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:43.000
King e4, best move.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:45.000
Okay, so we could play

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.000
f5 check. See, again, we could play

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:49.000
this move or we could not play this move.

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.000
I don't think there's a hurry to play f5

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:53.000
because we could always play it on the next move.

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.000
Again, white cannot deep freeze us anymore

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.000
because we played this move, h6.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:59.000
So let's see what happens.

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:01.000
We played this move, h6.

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:03.000
So,

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:05.000
broadly, our plan is now

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:07.000
to exploit our pawn majority, f4.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:09.000
So white is trying to go g5 here

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.000
so we shouldn't fall asleep. Although g5 could

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:13.000
be met with h5, but I still don't like that

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:15.000
