00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:03.000
I think we need to make one more move

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:05.000
before we are ready to start pushing

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:07.000
the h-pawn. This might be a draw.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.000
I mean, we're basically playing

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.000
at this point for two results, which is really

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:13.000
a pleasant feeling.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:15.000
But still, if white defends this accurately,

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.000
I think he should be able to draw this.

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:19.000
Yeah, king f6. And king f6 is a

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:21.000
pretty intuitive move for experienced endgame

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:23.000
players. There are several ideas of this move.

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.000
One of them is finally

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:27.000
to open up the pathway for the rook to

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.000
drop to h7, which is the best square on the board.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:31.000
This is why, you know,

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:33.000
your rook can make or break your endgame

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:35.000
experience, because on a square like h7

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:37.000
it's both attacking and defending at the

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.000
same time. The second thing that we should

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:41.000
consider doing is pushing while we have a

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:43.000
chance. Because here's the thing.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.000
After h5, king f3,

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:47.000
h4, our pawn

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:49.000
is itself cutting off white's

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:51.000
king from its ability to stop

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:53.000
that pawn by stepping in front of it. So

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:55.000
white could go king g2,

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:57.000
rook h7, king h3.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:59.000
But then perhaps we create a second

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:01.000
pass pawn with e5. So we want to

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.000
make this move e5, but we want to

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:05.000
do it at a strategic moment.

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:07.000
So I think we should start

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.000
by pushing h5,

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:11.000
drive this pawn up as far as it'll

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.000
go, put the rook behind it,

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:15.000
or maybe play e5 on the next move or something like

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.000
that. But e5 is not going anywhere

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:19.000
for the time being.

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.000
I think white's best move, and this is

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:23.000
an incredibly advanced move, is

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.000
actually king f2.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:27.000
Okay.

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:29.000
Let's think about this. h4,

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:31.000
king g2,

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:33.000
g3.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.000
There's so much calculation to do here.

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:37.000
h4,

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:39.000
king g2,

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:43.000
e5,

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:45.000
kxdx,

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:47.000
e3,

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:49.000
cut him off. I should be winning here.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.000
Should be winning here. I think

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:53.000
we should go

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:55.000
h4.

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:57.000
He's probably going to go king g2.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.000
And then I think we should go e5.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:01.000
I think that's the right moment to play e5.

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:05.000
Now he goes rook h3. That should be a mistake.

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.000
Now we have a check on d2 here.

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.000
Oh, but he's out-calculated me.

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:11.000
We can also go rook h7

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:13.000
by the way, which is a backup option.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:15.000
Rook d2, king e3,

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:17.000
takes b2, rook takes there,

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:19.000
there, there,

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:21.000
there. That might be a draw

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:23.000
actually. So I think

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:25.000
man,

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:27.000
I missed that detail there

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.000
that I'll show you after the game. Let's go rook h7.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:31.000
Let's go rook h7.

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:33.000
And try to

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:35.000
put the rook behind the pawn. I mean, that's the obvious move.

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:37.000
Rook h7 is obvious. Of course,

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.000
there was also rook d2 check, which is what I was relying on.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:41.000
I don't think it works.

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:45.000
Again, I'm making these judgments fully intuitively.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:49.000
Yeah, I don't know. This guy is 24-20.

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:51.000
I mean, I would say he's IM strength

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:53.000
or maybe FM strength.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:55.000
I mean, I'm not playing perfectly,

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:57.000
but yeah, he's very, very strong.

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:59.000
But legit. I mean, I think he is legit.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.000
I just think he's

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:03.000
extremely underrated.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.000
No, I won't flag. I mean, I might not win this game,

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:07.000
but I won't flag. And now we play e5.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:09.000
So again, this was the last chance for us

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:11.000
to get on the train. Because if white

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:13.000
had been able to go king d4,

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:15.000
then we wouldn't have been able to

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.000
play e5. So we can't miss that opportunity.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:19.000
It should be winning for black.

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:21.000
I mean, definitely the position is winning.

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:23.000
Can I convert it with a minute on my clock?

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:25.000
Now, what is it that we want to achieve here?

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:27.000
Well, what we want to achieve

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:29.000
broadly is we want to force this

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:31.000
rook to move. Because every time

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:33.000
we force the rook to move, our

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.000
pawn is able to advance one square

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:37.000
further and tie the rook down even

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:39.000
further. Ultimately, we want a situation

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:41.000
where the pawn reaches h2

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:43.000
and white's rook is

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:45.000
relegated to h1.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:47.000
When that scenario occurs,

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:49.000
we can basically

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:51.000
use that pawn as a bargaining chip,

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:53.000
give it away, but give it

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:55.000
away in order to transition to a

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:57.000
pawn endgame. This is the standard technique for winning

00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.000
such positions, where the rook

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:01.000
is behind the pawn. And then, in

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.000
the resulting scenario, white's king

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:05.000
is going to be, you know, in timber 2,

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:07.000
and we're going to converge

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:09.000
on these three pawns with our

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.000
own king. Sounds very

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:13.000
complicated, but I'll show you. Once I start showing

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:15.000
you this technique after the game,

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:17.000
you'll understand immediately what I'm talking

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:19.000
about.

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:21.000
Now, what

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:23.000
are the complicating factors here? Well, the issue

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:25.000
is that white is not obliged to move

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:27.000
the rook from h3. White has other moves. White is not

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:29.000
in zugzwang, in other words. White has

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:31.000
two types of moves that he can make.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:33.000
White can try to preemptively start pushing

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:35.000
the pawns, which is, I think, the most challenging, and of course

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:37.000
our opponent plays it.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:39.000
Now we have to decide

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:41.000
whether we're slowing white down

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.000
or whether we're pushing. And these

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:45.000
are very hard decisions to make.

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.000
They require tremendous

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:49.000
calculation.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:51.000
So there's

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.000
Kd5 there, there.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:55.000
It's a weird

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:57.000
construction, but in my view, what we need...

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:01.000
Okay, let's go Kd5.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.000
Let's go Kd5.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:07.000
Of course,

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:09.000
it is possible. And now Rh5.

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:11.000
This is the construction that I came up with. This actually

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:13.000
might be a genius idea. Look at this

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:15.000
rook. It's doing three

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:17.000
things. It's guarding h4 and f5,

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:19.000
and it's preventing white's king

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:21.000
from infiltrating further. So it's keeping the king at

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.000
bay, and it's keeping the rook at

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:25.000
bay indirectly, because any time the rook

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:27.000
moves, our pawn moves forward.

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:29.000
But I'm delegating to my

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:31.000
king a very important responsibility. I'm

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:33.000
basically leaving the king on its

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:35.000
own, and our king's job

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:37.000
is going to be twofold. I mean, first of all,

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:39.000
it not only has to keep white's pawn majority

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.000
restrained. That's not enough. Remember

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:43.000
our ultimate goal here, which is to

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.000
essentially induce Zugzwang

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:47.000
and force white to

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:49.000
move this rook away from h3

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:51.000
so that we can start pushing our h-pawn.

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:53.000
How are we going

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:55.000
to induce that scenario? We're going to

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.000
basically try to freeze white's pawn majority.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.000
And we've already

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:01.000
come very, very close to achieving that, because

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:03.000
b5 is impossible, because

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:05.000
if king takes c5.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:07.000
So white can push the a-pawn, but we

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:09.000
can basically wait it out. After a4,

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.000
we can play a6.

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:13.000
And then if white plays a5,

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:15.000
then he basically deep freezes his

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:17.000
own pawn majority, and then we've

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.000
clearly succeeded, right?

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:21.000
So the only other option

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:23.000
is for white to drop the king back.

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.000
Clearly that signifies progress,

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:27.000
and then we can use our own king

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:29.000
to infiltrate and start posing

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:31.000
threats against white's pawn majority.

