where you develop your pieces and white

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:15.000
just totally collapses in the center.

00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:17.000
In fact, this is what happens in

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:19.000
most games featuring intermediate players

00:48:19.000 --> 00:48:21.000
where black is well-versed in

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:23.000
tricky accelerated lines.

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:25.000
To get to the point, knight f6

00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:27.000
is tricky and we've already played this

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:29.000
once, I think. In that game, our

00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:31.000
opponent immediately fell into their first trap

00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:33.000
which is to take on c6 and to play

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:35.000
e5. This is a very tempting move

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:37.000
but any time you have this situation

00:48:37.000 --> 00:48:39.000
you have to, of course, remember

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:41.000
that we have queen a5 check.

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:43.000
Don't forget this trap. This is

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:45.000
super important. Of course,

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:47.000
we pick up the pawn on e5 and here black is

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:49.000
easily a healthy pawn up and then

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:51.000
we go bishop g7, we castle

00:48:51.000 --> 00:48:53.000
and we can easily advance d5. The queen

00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:55.000
can always drop back to this envelope

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:57.000
on c7. It's also common in the Scandi.

00:48:57.000 --> 00:48:59.000
So we can chill on e5

00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:01.000
but then when white plays

00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:03.000
for instance, rook e1,

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:05.000
we don't have to but an easy way

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:07.000
to guarantee safety is to play queen c7.

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:09.000
So that

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:11.000
doesn't work. Now if white takes

00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:13.000
and then goes knight c3,

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:15.000
then, as I've explained many times before,

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.000
this trade favors black. This c6

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:19.000
pawn is now a great positional asset because

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:21.000
it's holding this knight at bay and we've

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:23.000
got this semi-open b-file

00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:25.000
and this is just a very comfortable position for black

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:27.000
as well. So the main

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:29.000
move here is knight to c3. f3 I think

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:31.000
is already a serious inaccuracy and I will

00:49:31.000 --> 00:49:33.000
check the best way to fight against it. I've never

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:35.000
analyzed f3 so

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:37.000
maybe knight takes d4

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:39.000
is an inaccuracy in response.

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:43.000
So knight c3, my recommended

00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:45.000
line, is knight takes d4, queen

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:47.000
takes d4. Now not

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:49.000
bishop g7 because here e5

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:51.000
is very dangerous but instead

00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:53.000
you have to remember to play d6.

00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:55.000
Super, super tricky sideline.

00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:57.000
Now, to

00:49:57.000 --> 00:49:59.000
explain why it's a tricky sideline, first of all

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:01.000
the sort of important thing to

00:50:01.000 --> 00:50:03.000
understand is that e5 is no longer dangerous

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:05.000
because after we take on e5,

00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:07.000
white can take on d8. Yes,

00:50:07.000 --> 00:50:09.000
we lose castling rights but the

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:11.000
reality is that white

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:13.000
does not garner sufficient compensation for the

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:15.000
pawn. And you should remember

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:17.000
in such positions, I know I make

00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:19.000
fun of that phrase, I'm going to verify

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:21.000
this with the engine.

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:23.000
What a lot of people are afraid of in these types

00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:25.000
of situations is

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:27.000
let's say a move like bishop to e3

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:29.000
and white's going to castle queenside

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:31.000
and it's going to be a check.

00:50:31.000 --> 00:50:33.000
But that's just not that dangerous. That engine move

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:35.000
here in fact is just to ignore

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:37.000
everything and hit the bishop with knight g4.

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:39.000
And big deal,

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:41.000
white castles queenside, you block with the bishop.

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:43.000
Yeah, you're self-pinning

00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:45.000
but white has no way to put pressure on this bishop.

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:47.000
You might say, well, what about this move

00:50:47.000 --> 00:50:49.000
threatening bishop b5? Again,

00:50:49.000 --> 00:50:51.000
you should not be afraid, particularly

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:53.000
at this level, of one-move threats.

00:50:53.000 --> 00:50:55.000
They're not dangerous. After

00:50:55.000 --> 00:50:57.000
knight takes e3, f takes e3, the engine

00:50:57.000 --> 00:50:59.000
finds a brilliant way to

00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:01.000
defend against the threat. Bishop h6

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:03.000
is possible, and who can tell me,

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:05.000
if you're watching on YouTube, pause and figure this out,

00:51:05.000 --> 00:51:07.000
why does bishop b5 not win a piece?

00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:09.000
Easy but very pretty.

00:51:11.000 --> 00:51:13.000
What's America Marty Wolfman for the subs?

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:15.000
Yeah, bishop

00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:17.000
takes e3, check. The bishop drops back

00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:19.000
to d4 and intercepts

00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:21.000
the rook. Incredible.

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:23.000
And black emerges with two extra

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:25.000
pawns. Now apparently this position

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:27.000
is still equal, but white has to play

00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:29.000
this like an engine. Practically speaking,

00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:31.000
I think black is doing great here.

00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:33.000
The more common move is going to be queen

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:35.000
takes e5, but again, obviously this is very good

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:37.000
for black because the center has opened.

00:51:37.000 --> 00:51:39.000
White's got weaknesses on the d-file. The queen

00:51:39.000 --> 00:51:41.000
is now very vulnerable, and our next

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:43.000
move is going to be castle.

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:45.000
You can basically play this without analysis,

00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:47.000
but to give you a sample

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:49.000
line, and there's no question

00:51:49.000 --> 00:51:51.000
that queen takes queen is a better move than

00:51:51.000 --> 00:51:53.000
queen takes e5. If white plays bishop

00:51:53.000 --> 00:51:55.000
e2, we castle. White

00:51:55.000 --> 00:51:57.000
castles, and one important

00:51:57.000 --> 00:51:59.000
point about playing

00:51:59.000 --> 00:52:01.000
these types of accelerated positions as we did

00:52:01.000 --> 00:52:03.000
in the game is not to rush to try

00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:05.000
to give a discovered attack to the queen.

00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:07.000
A lot of you might be tempted by a move

00:52:07.000 --> 00:52:09.000
like knight g4. Again,

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:11.000
this is one moveitis.

00:52:11.000 --> 00:52:13.000
A move is not strong because it poses a threat.

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:15.000
In fact, it helps the queen get back

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:17.000
to a safer square,

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:19.000
and if you get too obsessed with this, you're going

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:21.000
to end up paying the price for

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:23.000
these myopic moves.

00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:25.000
Instead, in the spirit

00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:27.000
of what we did in the game, the best move is bishop

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:29.000
to e6. Again, just developing

00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:31.000
the bishop, and actually this now

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:33.000
poses a threat of a discovered

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:35.000
attack. So if white sleeps through

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:37.000
this moment, then we have this very powerful

00:52:37.000 --> 00:52:39.000
move, knight to d5, and we're

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:41.000
able to take on c3, or even to

00:52:41.000 --> 00:52:43.000
garner the bishop pair with knight takes e3.

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:45.000
If white plays rook d1, again,

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:47.000
it's just a one-move threat. We can still

00:52:47.000 --> 00:52:49.000
play knight d5, which is

00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:51.000
a brazen-looking move, but we hit the queen,

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:53.000
and the point is that after queen

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:55.000
g3, we can solve our problems

00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:57.000
tactically with knight takes knight,

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:59.000
and if white plays rook takes d8,

00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:01.000
then knight takes c2 check picks up the queen

00:53:01.000 --> 00:53:03.000
and then picks up the rook.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:05.000
Of course, white has to play

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:07.000
bc, and we develop

00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:09.000
the queen to a5, and black is much better.

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:11.000
So you can dig around here on your own if you want

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.000
a fuller understanding.

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:15.000
You're not going to face the movie 5 that often,

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:17.000
though. White

00:53:17.000 --> 00:53:19.000
has many setups here, and I could spend an hour just

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:21.000
talking about the theory of this line.

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:23.000
But let me show you what happens in

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:25.000
most games at sort of the lower levels.

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:27.000
I can check Leech S for the most common move.

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:29.000
But most people, they basically

00:53:29.000 --> 00:53:31.000
just develop their pieces. They play bishop

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:33.000
e2. When I faced this line with white for

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:35.000
the first time, this is also what I did.

00:53:35.000 --> 00:53:37.000
Bishop e2 and bishop e3 is a

00:53:37.000 --> 00:53:39.000
super common setup. It's not bad. I mean,

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:41.000
it's one of the main lines, but it's not the most ambitious

00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:43.000
either. Now after castles,

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:45.000
we reach the first important moment.

00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:47.000
A lot of people castle here. Black is

00:53:47.000 --> 00:53:49.000
already better. What should black

00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:51.000
do, who can tell me, after

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:53.000
white castles? You should not be looking for a tactic

00:53:53.000 --> 00:53:55.000
here. You should be looking for a

00:53:55.000 --> 00:53:57.000
positionally improving trade.

00:53:57.000 --> 00:53:59.000
Not Nd7.

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:01.000
Not b6.

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:03.000
So I've scared you away from

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:05.000
delivering discovered attacks, but here you

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:07.000
do have a good discovered attack, Ng4.

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:09.000
Why? Well, because

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:11.000
we're able to pick off the bishop. Why does that

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:13.000
benefit black? Well, it benefits black pretty

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:15.000
intuitively, because now we have this uncontested

00:54:15.000 --> 00:54:17.000
dark-squared bishop, and look at

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:19.000
how weak white's dark-squared bishops

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:21.000
are. Hopefully you're already gaining enough of an understanding

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:23.000
to know what the next move

00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:25.000
is likely to be. Where does

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:27.000
the bishop on c8 belong?

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:29.000
Well, it belongs on e6,

00:54:29.000 --> 00:54:31.000
hitting the c4-pawn.

00:54:31.000 --> 00:54:33.000
Now let's say that white plays a move

00:54:33.000 --> 00:54:35.000
like b3. Well, now

00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:37.000
we have actually a very powerful

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:39.000
move, Qb6,

00:54:39.000 --> 00:54:41.000
which is another sexy idea. Very

00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:43.000
sexy idea. And in the event

00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:45.000
of the queen trade, we open up the a-file,

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:47.000
and we have an attack on c3.

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:49.000
Let's say white defends.

00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:51.000
Now we can play even the move

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:53.000
b5, which really shows you

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:55.000
the power of the accelerated when it

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:57.000
fires on all cylinders. Of course, it's a pretty cooperative

00:54:57.000 --> 00:54:59.000
line, but here white can essentially

00:54:59.000 --> 00:55:01.000
resign, because he's losing material.

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:03.000
Right, so it's this

00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:05.000
power of the bishops,

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:07.000
and

00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:09.000
definitely white has to move the queen for that

00:55:09.000 --> 00:55:11.000
reason, so Qd2 is the main move, and the best

00:55:11.000 --> 00:55:13.000
move. You know, once you have your feet

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:15.000
under you, the moves become quite easy.

00:55:15.000 --> 00:55:17.000
Here you play Bb6,

00:55:17.000 --> 00:55:19.000
and develop your bishop. And most

00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:21.000
people in this position, of course, they castle kings.

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:23.000
If white analyzes this with an engine, then he

00:55:23.000 --> 00:55:25.000
can potentially play better. Now there are several moves

00:55:25.000 --> 00:55:27.000
in this position that you can try,

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:29.000
but the main move is Qa5.

00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:31.000
You basically bring the queen out,

00:55:31.000 --> 00:55:33.000
and put more pressure on white's queen side.

00:55:33.000 --> 00:55:35.000
And I claim that this position is very

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:37.000
very hard to play for white, if he's

00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:39.000
not very well versed in the theory.

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:41.000
So a couple of

00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:43.000
examples to illustrate how

00:55:43.000 --> 00:55:45.000
dangerous this can be. A lot of people get very

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:47.000
tempted by this tactic, Nd5,

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:49.000
which is a common tactic, but it doesn't work here,

00:55:49.000 --> 00:55:51.000
because after the take, trade

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:53.000
on d2. White takes on e7, intermezzo,

00:55:53.000 --> 00:55:55.000
but the line doesn't end here.

00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:57.000
Black plays Nxc4, and

00:55:57.000 --> 00:55:59.000
develops a huge initiative. The knight is stranded,

00:55:59.000 --> 00:56:01.000
everything's hanging, this is better for black.

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:03.000
So that's one line

00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:05.000
to remember. Now, if white

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.000
plays b3, because a lot of people are tempted

00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:09.000
to reinforce

00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:11.000
the c4 pawn, b3 is a good move.

00:56:11.000 --> 00:56:13.000
And black has a couple of options

00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:15.000
here, but the best is to

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:17.000
play Rf to c8.

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:19.000
Again, let's say white plays

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:21.000
a move like f3 and sleepwalks

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:23.000
through this moment. Well, already

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:25.000
black develops a huge initiative

00:56:25.000 --> 00:56:27.000
on the queen side by playing a6.

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:29.000
What are we preparing? We're

00:56:29.000 --> 00:56:31.000
preparing the move b5. Why is that

00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:33.000
move dangerous? Because after b5, white is unable

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:35.000
to take, because the knight on c3

00:56:35.000 --> 00:56:37.000
hangs. So let's say white says,

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:39.000
okay, well I'm going to go Rc1, and I'm going to defend

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:41.000
this knight. Well, it turns out that black can still

00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:43.000
very often play b5.

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:45.000
Because after a cb, ab,

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:47.000
white collapses on the

00:56:47.000 --> 00:56:49.000
queen side. If bishop takes b5,

00:56:49.000 --> 00:56:51.000
who can tell me the simple and very

00:56:51.000 --> 00:56:53.000
powerful move that makes this line work?

00:56:53.000 --> 00:56:55.000
This is part of the big reason

00:56:55.000 --> 00:56:57.000
that you put your rook on c8.

00:56:57.000 --> 00:56:59.000
Of course, rook takes knight.

00:56:59.000 --> 00:57:01.000
And as I've explained many times,

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:03.000
two pieces

00:57:03.000 --> 00:57:05.000
for a rook usually benefits the minor pieces,

00:57:05.000 --> 00:57:07.000
particularly in the middle game

00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:09.000
with a wide open center. By the way,

00:57:09.000 --> 00:57:11.000
after rook takes c3, we also

00:57:11.000 --> 00:57:13.000
have the move knight takes e4,

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:15.000
which is even sexier than queen takes b5,

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.000
because we recapture the rook,

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:19.000
and everything starts hanging. You can

00:57:19.000 --> 00:57:21.000
analyze this a little bit more

00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:23.000
on your own. And if white plays knight takes b5,

00:57:23.000 --> 00:57:25.000
then of course we take the queen.

00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:27.000
If white plays

00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:29.000
rook takes c8 check, we can do

00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:31.000
either rook takes c8 or even bishop

00:57:31.000 --> 00:57:33.000
takes c8, and we recapture

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:35.000
on a2 with a huge initiative.

00:57:35.000 --> 00:57:37.000
In the endgame, after rook d1, we can redeploy

00:57:37.000 --> 00:57:39.000
the bishop on e6, and start

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:41.000
tackling this pawn. White is getting tortured here.

00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:43.000
So that's

00:57:43.000 --> 00:57:45.000
yet another line.

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:47.000
Now, of course, white can play a4

00:57:47.000 --> 00:57:49.000
and defend against b5.

00:57:49.000 --> 00:57:51.000
Now you should not play b5

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:53.000
because of ab with a discovered attack

00:57:53.000 --> 00:57:55.000
on the queen. But any time a4

00:57:55.000 --> 00:57:57.000
is played on the accelerated dragon, you should

00:57:57.000 --> 00:57:59.000
understand that the b3 pawn becomes

00:57:59.000 --> 00:58:01.000
a permanent weakness. What are the tools

00:58:01.000 --> 00:58:03.000
available to us to attack it? Well, one

00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:05.000
thing we can do is just stick the queen

00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:07.000
on this weak square. White

00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:09.000
is forced to defend the pawn, but here you have to

00:58:09.000 --> 00:58:11.000
remember the maneuver knight f6 to d7.

00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:13.000
Some of you were suggesting this move earlier.

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:15.000
Here it's actually very strong

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:17.000
because it opens up an attack on the knight, and the

00:58:17.000 --> 00:58:19.000
knight potentially dives into c5.

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:21.000
And also, after rook

00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:23.000
fc1, we can now play b5.

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:25.000
ab, ab.

00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:27.000
And

00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:29.000
if cb, then we win

00:58:29.000 --> 00:58:31.000
the knight. And if

00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:33.000
knight takes b5, who can tell me

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:35.000
what is the correct sequence

00:58:35.000 --> 00:58:37.000
from this position?

00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:39.000
This will test if you understand kind of the

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:41.000
idea of b5. I'll be right back.

00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:43.000
What's the move here?

00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:47.000
Very good. You got it.

00:58:47.000 --> 00:58:49.000
Now the key thing to understand

00:58:49.000 --> 00:58:51.000
is that very often when you sack a pawn,

00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:53.000
you should not rule out a queen trade.

00:58:53.000 --> 00:58:55.000
Queen trade doesn't mean anything.

00:58:55.000 --> 00:58:57.000
This skewers the bishops.

00:58:57.000 --> 00:58:59.000
Now after rook d1,

00:58:59.000 --> 00:59:01.000
it's time to start a fire on the other

00:59:01.000 --> 00:59:03.000
side of the board. We have

00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:05.000
white very much compromised, but

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:07.000
in order to

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:09.000
translate that to an actual

00:59:09.000 --> 00:59:11.000
advantage, we have to continually

00:59:11.000 --> 00:59:13.000
create threats and challenge white

00:59:13.000 --> 00:59:15.000
where he's weak. And in order to do

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:17.000
that, we often have to use our pawn. So this is

00:59:17.000 --> 00:59:19.000
another very typical Ixari to dragon idea.

00:59:19.000 --> 00:59:21.000
A very timely use of the move

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:23.000
f7, f5.

00:59:23.000 --> 00:59:25.000
What is the point of this move? Well, if white

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:27.000
takes on f5, then you play bishop

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:29.000
f5, rook c1. And you can

00:59:29.000 --> 00:59:31.000
go either bishop b2 to trap

00:59:31.000 --> 00:59:33.000
the rook or knight to c5 when white has

00:59:33.000 --> 00:59:35.000
no good way to defend b3. Just look at the

00:59:35.000 --> 00:59:37.000
extent of black's domination here.

00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:39.000
And you might say, well, I don't understand.

00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:41.000
What if white ignores? Well, it's not that

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:43.000
easy to make a move here because white is so

00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:45.000
paralyzed. But let's say white

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:47.000
literally plays h3.

00:59:47.000 --> 00:59:49.000
Well, now we can play knight c5.

00:59:49.000 --> 00:59:51.000
And we're already attacking the e4

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:53.000
pawn. And white starts to collapse here.

00:59:53.000 --> 00:59:55.000
White can play b4 to chase the knight away,

00:59:55.000 --> 00:59:57.000
but now the knight squeezes into a4

00:59:57.000 --> 00:59:59.000
and now c4 is weak. And white

00:59:59.000 --> 01:00:01.000
can't move this rook because the bishop hangs.

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:03.000
So, much like the Grunfeld,

01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:05.000
the accelerated is about appreciating

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:07.000
the power of piece

01:00:07.000 --> 01:00:09.000
activity and the ability

01:00:09.000 --> 01:00:11.000
to sacrifice sometimes a pawn, sometimes

01:00:11.000 --> 01:00:13.000
even more than that, to achieve this

01:00:13.000 --> 01:00:15.000
long-term activity and to understand

01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:17.000
that it's not all about—when you sacrifice

01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:19.000
something, it's not like you have to win immediately,

01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:21.000
right, or win in two or three moves.

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:23.000
You can play for a very prolonged period of time,

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:25.000
let's say, down a pawn, even down a piece,

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:27.000
as long as your other pieces are active.

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:29.000
This is the concept of squeezing

01:00:29.000 --> 01:00:31.000
and putting pressure, which is very challenging,

01:00:31.000 --> 01:00:33.000
but it's why I'm talking about it,

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:35.000
because we're at a high level now.

01:00:35.000 --> 01:00:37.000
Okay, so that was a lot.

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:39.000
Now, there are many

01:00:39.000 --> 01:00:41.000
more lines to show here. I will

01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:43.000
point out that I don't remember what the—

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:45.000
what Wesley's recommendation

01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:47.000
is in his e4 course, like

01:00:47.000 --> 01:00:49.000
you guys can tell me, but the main

01:00:49.000 --> 01:00:51.000
line, bishop e2, bishop e7, is bishop e3.

01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:53.000
The line that used to be

01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:55.000
considered best is actually

01:00:55.000 --> 01:00:57.000
bishop c1 to g5. At one point,

01:00:57.000 --> 01:00:59.000
I think this was considered to be

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:01.000
a very good line. Here,

01:01:01.000 --> 01:01:03.000
in contrast, of course, black

01:01:03.000 --> 01:01:05.000
has no good discovery because this bishop

01:01:05.000 --> 01:01:07.000
is invulnerable,

01:01:07.000 --> 01:01:09.000
but our strategy barely changes.

01:01:09.000 --> 01:01:11.000
We still play bishop e6. We're still

01:01:11.000 --> 01:01:13.000
threatening knight d5, so white

01:01:13.000 --> 01:01:15.000
is now forced to drop back to d2.

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:17.000
And now we play

01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:19.000
queen a5 once again. We basically

01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:21.000
make the same moves against everything.

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:23.000
And another very important thing to understand

01:01:23.000 --> 01:01:25.000
is that very often,

01:01:25.000 --> 01:01:27.000
white plays bishop h6 in these types of positions.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:29.000
And this is a bad move.

01:01:29.000 --> 01:01:31.000
And the reason why is that you do

01:01:31.000 --> 01:01:33.000
give up your fianchettoed bishop, but look at

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:35.000
how weak these pawns are. The simplest

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:37.000
way to pick them off is to play rook a c8.

01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:39.000
And white can't play

01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:41.000
b3, a very common blunder at a lower level.

01:01:41.000 --> 01:01:43.000
Even some GMs have blundered like this

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:45.000
against me. White can

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:47.000
play knight d5 and hit e7,

01:01:47.000 --> 01:01:49.000
but after bishop takes d5,

01:01:49.000 --> 01:01:51.000
if white takes with a c-pawn, the

01:01:51.000 --> 01:01:53.000
e-pawn drops, and if white takes to the e-pawn,

01:01:53.000 --> 01:01:55.000
this is another classic scenario.

01:01:55.000 --> 01:01:57.000
This is a pawn chain. How do we attack

01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.000
this pawn chain? Well, you already know that b5

01:01:59.000 --> 01:02:01.000
is one of black's main tools

01:02:01.000 --> 01:02:03.000
on the queen side. And after c takes b5,

01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:05.000
you can take this pawn, or

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:07.000
you can put the emphasis on

01:02:07.000 --> 01:02:09.000
peace activity and infiltrate to c2.

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:11.000
Black is the only one having fun.

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:15.000
So,

01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:17.000
King's Gambit asks,

01:02:17.000 --> 01:02:19.000
what if white pushes f4?

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:21.000
f4 does not create a threat,

01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:23.000
and so we can simply continue with our campaign

01:02:23.000 --> 01:02:25.000
and play rook a c8. And after b3,

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:27.000
you guys should already understand

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:29.000
what black's move is.

01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:31.000
When b3 is played, the first thing you should always test

01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:33.000
is whether you can play the immediate

01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:35.000
b5. And here you can,

01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.000
because the queen on d2

01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:39.000
is hanging. The move f4 has

01:02:39.000 --> 01:02:41.000
severed the connection between the

01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:43.000
bishop and the queen, making b5 possible.

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:47.000
There are so many of these lines that I can show you,

01:02:47.000 --> 01:02:49.000
but I think that's enough for now. This is like

01:02:49.000 --> 01:02:51.000
a good introduction to this line.

01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:53.000
I'm sure that we're going to have another game

01:02:53.000 --> 01:02:55.000
when we're 2200 or so, and then I'll talk more

01:02:55.000 --> 01:02:57.000
about theory.

01:02:57.000 --> 01:02:59.000
Yeah, knight takes e4 was not possible.

01:02:59.000 --> 01:03:01.000
And the reason is,

01:03:01.000 --> 01:03:03.000
sorry, I just wanted to address

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:05.000
this question. So we were talking about

01:03:05.000 --> 01:03:07.000
this line, castles castles, bishop b6,

01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:09.000
queen d2, queen a5,

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:11.000
f4,

01:03:11.000 --> 01:03:13.000
rook a c8, b3. Knight takes

01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:15.000
e4 is bad, because white takes on e4.

01:03:15.000 --> 01:03:17.000
And when

01:03:17.000 --> 01:03:19.000
the smoke clears, white has

01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:21.000
two pieces for a rook. Now, black

01:03:21.000 --> 01:03:23.000
is all right here, but this is nothing

01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:25.000
to write home about. Really nothing to

01:03:25.000 --> 01:03:27.000
write home about, because black's rooks are also very, very

01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:29.000
bad. There are no open files to

01:03:29.000 --> 01:03:31.000
control. And then you might

01:03:31.000 --> 01:03:33.000
say, well, but two pieces for a rook, how do

01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:35.000
I understand who's better? That's

01:03:35.000 --> 01:03:37.000
one of those things that is decided on a

01:03:37.000 --> 01:03:39.000
case-by-case basis. It's an imbalance.

01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:41.000
And that is why one of the biggest pieces of

01:03:41.000 --> 01:03:43.000
advice given to higher-rated players is

01:03:43.000 --> 01:03:45.000
to seek positions with a lot of imbalances,

01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:47.000
because they are harder

01:03:47.000 --> 01:03:49.000
for a lower-rated player to evaluate,

01:03:49.000 --> 01:03:51.000
because they require a lot of experience

01:03:51.000 --> 01:03:53.000
and understanding to

01:03:53.000 --> 01:03:55.000
evaluate properly.

01:03:55.000 --> 01:03:57.000
Okay, so you shouldn't be thinking of this in the terms

01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:59.000
of, oh, what is the formula

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:01.000
for evaluating this? Two pieces for

01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:03.000
a rook is generally good for the two pieces,

01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:05.000
so most of the time

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:07.000
you should have a good justification for going

01:04:07.000 --> 01:04:09.000
for it if you are the side of the rook.

01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:11.000
Okay.

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:13.000
Hopefully that was halfway

01:04:13.000 --> 01:04:15.000
sensible. Again, I'll talk

01:04:15.000 --> 01:04:17.000
more theory later. Let's address our

01:04:17.000 --> 01:04:19.000
opponent's move, f3. I'm going to check

01:04:19.000 --> 01:04:21.000
this with the engine. Of course, f3 has been

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:23.000
played many, many times.

01:04:23.000 --> 01:04:25.000
Oh, so there's some tactics

01:04:25.000 --> 01:04:27.000
here that are interesting.

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:29.000
Yeah, so apparently

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:31.000
Nxd4 is all right,

01:04:31.000 --> 01:04:33.000
but not the best move.

01:04:33.000 --> 01:04:35.000
The best move, according to the engine, is

01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:37.000
indeed Bg7.

01:04:37.000 --> 01:04:39.000
White plays Bb3.

01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:41.000
And here we have to

01:04:41.000 --> 01:04:43.000
rely on a

01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:45.000
trap.

01:04:45.000 --> 01:04:47.000
I mean, in order to

01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:49.000
understand this move,

01:04:49.000 --> 01:04:51.000
you have to know the most famous

01:04:51.000 --> 01:04:53.000
accelerated dragon trap,

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:55.000
which I've shown before. We've actually

01:04:55.000 --> 01:04:57.000
already had it. That trap

01:04:57.000 --> 01:04:59.000
occurs when White basically

01:04:59.000 --> 01:05:01.000
tries to play it like a regular

01:05:01.000 --> 01:05:03.000
dragon. When White plays the move

01:05:03.000 --> 01:05:05.000
Bc4,

01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:07.000
we castle and White basically tries

01:05:07.000 --> 01:05:09.000
to make all the moves that he would make in a dragon.

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:11.000
Who remembers what the

01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:13.000
punishment is against this line? What's the famous

01:05:13.000 --> 01:05:15.000
trap that Bobby Fischer fell into

01:05:15.000 --> 01:05:17.000
against Oscar Pano? It's not

01:05:17.000 --> 01:05:19.000
Nxd4. That blunders a piece, but you want to

01:05:19.000 --> 01:05:21.000
set that move up with a very

01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:23.000
counterintuitive move. And that counterintuitive

01:05:23.000 --> 01:05:25.000
move is Qb6. And the whole

01:05:25.000 --> 01:05:27.000
point is that the bishop on e3 is

01:05:27.000 --> 01:05:29.000
undefended. So White would normally

01:05:29.000 --> 01:05:31.000
be able to do something like this. Here,

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:33.000
of course, White's not able to do that.

01:05:33.000 --> 01:05:35.000
Now, the common response is Nf5.

01:05:35.000 --> 01:05:37.000
But now we grab this pawn on b2

01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:39.000
and Black wins the game.

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:41.000
And the reason why... Let me refresh because I

01:05:41.000 --> 01:05:43.000
got disconnected. One sec. Sorry.

01:05:43.000 --> 01:05:45.000
And the reason why...

01:05:47.000 --> 01:05:49.000
And this is important. I know this is a little bit

01:05:49.000 --> 01:05:51.000
tedious. You might be yawning if you're watching on YouTube.

01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:53.000
But you have to build

01:05:53.000 --> 01:05:55.000
the foundations of opening knowledge.

01:05:55.000 --> 01:05:57.000
There's just no way around it.

01:05:57.000 --> 01:05:59.000
So Nf5, Qb2, hits

01:05:59.000 --> 01:06:01.000
the knight on c3. And not only

01:06:01.000 --> 01:06:03.000
that, we're also going to win one of the bishops.

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:05.000
So White loses a bunch of pieces

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:07.000
here. If White

01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:09.000
plays something like

01:06:09.000 --> 01:06:11.000
Bb3... Sorry.

01:06:11.000 --> 01:06:13.000
If White plays something like Bb3

01:06:13.000 --> 01:06:15.000
to defend the pawn, now

01:06:15.000 --> 01:06:17.000
you're able to play Nxc4. This looks

01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:19.000
very confusing, but in fact it's very easy to

01:06:19.000 --> 01:06:21.000
understand. You now have three

01:06:21.000 --> 01:06:23.000
attackers on this knight and only

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:25.000
two defenders. Once again,

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:27.000
this knight is very limited in its scope because

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:29.000
the bishop on e3 is going to drop.

01:06:29.000 --> 01:06:31.000
If White plays Nf5,

01:06:31.000 --> 01:06:33.000
then we can play Nxc3 and hit

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:35.000
the queen. And in this way, we

01:06:35.000 --> 01:06:37.000
evacuate the knight that's hanging.

01:06:37.000 --> 01:06:39.000
And if White plays Fe, then we

01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:41.000
play Bxd4. Specifically,

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:43.000
Bxd4 to not self-pin.

01:06:43.000 --> 01:06:45.000
And we're good. We're fine.

01:06:45.000 --> 01:06:47.000
We take with the queen, and we're simply

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:49.000
up a pawn.

01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:51.000
White can equalize here,

01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:53.000
but it's very hard.

01:06:53.000 --> 01:06:55.000
Fischer figured it out against Pano.

01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:57.000
So with

01:06:57.000 --> 01:06:59.000
this idea under your belt,

01:06:59.000 --> 01:07:01.000
it should be easier to understand what the move

01:07:01.000 --> 01:07:03.000
is here. Again, it's this queen b6 idea.

01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:05.000
Now here, it's even more complicated

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:07.000
because now Nf5 looks quite

01:07:07.000 --> 01:07:09.000
a bit scarier. The bishop on g7

01:07:09.000 --> 01:07:11.000
drops with check, but

01:07:11.000 --> 01:07:13.000
he did fall into the trap, but then Fischer was

01:07:13.000 --> 01:07:15.000
able to equalize.

01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:17.000
If you calculate further, you

01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:19.000
will notice that we quickly win the piece back

01:07:19.000 --> 01:07:21.000
because this knight is trapped and the rook

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:23.000
is hanging. So this move

01:07:23.000 --> 01:07:25.000
is not scary because

01:07:25.000 --> 01:07:27.000
White has no good discovery.

01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:29.000
This is a double-check big deal.

01:07:29.000 --> 01:07:31.000
We just go Kg8, and we win.

01:07:31.000 --> 01:07:33.000
It's scary, but if you know that this

01:07:33.000 --> 01:07:35.000
is the move, then it's easy.

01:07:35.000 --> 01:07:37.000
White has to defend the rook.

01:07:37.000 --> 01:07:39.000
Then we take on g7.

01:07:39.000 --> 01:07:41.000
And Black is up a pawn here.

01:07:43.000 --> 01:07:45.000
And I think practically this is a very

01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:47.000
hard position for White to play because

01:07:47.000 --> 01:07:49.000
if White plays a move like

01:07:49.000 --> 01:07:51.000
Bte2, which I think is the

01:07:51.000 --> 01:07:53.000
logical move, then we stick

01:07:53.000 --> 01:07:55.000
the knight right in White's grill, Nt4.

01:07:55.000 --> 01:07:57.000
We're threatening a fork.

01:07:57.000 --> 01:07:59.000
And if White just ignores

01:07:59.000 --> 01:08:01.000
and castles, then

01:08:01.000 --> 01:08:03.000
we actually can get away

01:08:03.000 --> 01:08:05.000
with Nc2.

01:08:05.000 --> 01:08:07.000
Because after Rb1, who can tell

01:08:07.000 --> 01:08:09.000
me what the key resource

01:08:09.000 --> 01:08:11.000
is for Black? And this is kind of a theme

01:08:11.000 --> 01:08:13.000
in these lines.

01:08:13.000 --> 01:08:15.000
Yeah, it's simple. Nxc3.

01:08:15.000 --> 01:08:17.000
And we take and hit the queen at the

01:08:17.000 --> 01:08:19.000
same time. Don't forget about these moves.

01:08:19.000 --> 01:08:21.000
And the key is not to automatically

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:23.000
assume, oh, I have to move my queen.

01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:25.000
You have to look for moves that

01:08:25.000 --> 01:08:27.000
let's say take a piece and simultaneously

01:08:27.000 --> 01:08:29.000
attack your opponent's piece.

01:08:29.000 --> 01:08:31.000
And when the smoke clears,

