01:16:29.000 --> 01:16:31.000
Now, this is a very important

01:16:31.000 --> 01:16:33.000
type of tactic which occurs in

01:16:33.000 --> 01:16:35.000
a different version.

01:16:35.000 --> 01:16:37.000
I've seen this tactic many times.

01:16:37.000 --> 01:16:39.000
Let me search up a sample

01:16:39.000 --> 01:16:41.000
to help you understand

01:16:41.000 --> 01:16:43.000
what I'm talking about.

01:16:43.000 --> 01:16:45.000
Let me see if I can quickly and efficiently

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:47.000
find an example.

01:16:47.000 --> 01:16:49.000
So,

01:16:49.000 --> 01:16:51.000
it's a tactic that relates specifically

01:16:51.000 --> 01:16:53.000
to scenarios where the bishop is on e6,

01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:55.000
which happens quite often, because e6 is a

01:16:55.000 --> 01:16:57.000
standard developing square for the bishop. This is just

01:16:57.000 --> 01:16:59.000
some game between

01:16:59.000 --> 01:17:01.000
two 2,000s.

01:17:01.000 --> 01:17:03.000
And White finds the following idea.

01:17:03.000 --> 01:17:05.000
White plays Ra to e1,

01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:07.000
forcing

01:17:07.000 --> 01:17:09.000
the bishop on e6. And now White finds a very

01:17:09.000 --> 01:17:11.000
powerful move. I think a lot of you

01:17:11.000 --> 01:17:13.000
would be very tempted to take this

01:17:13.000 --> 01:17:15.000
one, but this cashes in too soon

01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:17.000
because Black moves the rook and Black is holding on.

01:17:17.000 --> 01:17:19.000
White plays Qe3,

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:21.000
a very powerful move, and Black is powerless.

01:17:21.000 --> 01:17:23.000
Black tries to get the queen

01:17:23.000 --> 01:17:25.000
out of the pin, and

01:17:25.000 --> 01:17:27.000
the key, Qe5, threatening

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:29.000
mate, and in order to defend

01:17:29.000 --> 01:17:31.000
against the mate, Black has to push the f-pawn,

01:17:31.000 --> 01:17:33.000
which relinquishes control over the bishop.

01:17:33.000 --> 01:17:35.000
Okay? And that's

01:17:35.000 --> 01:17:37.000
the general gist

01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:39.000
of what I'm talking about.

01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:41.000
Now let's go back to the game.

01:17:41.000 --> 01:17:43.000
Yeah, so same concept, right?

01:17:43.000 --> 01:17:45.000
You give that check, and in order to defend against

01:17:45.000 --> 01:17:47.000
the check, you have to relinquish the control over the bishop.

01:17:47.000 --> 01:17:49.000
Something very similar

01:17:49.000 --> 01:17:51.000
could happen in a scenario

01:17:51.000 --> 01:17:53.000
by the way like this, where

01:17:53.000 --> 01:17:55.000
again, you're threatening mate. To defend against

01:17:55.000 --> 01:17:57.000
mate, you have to give up a land piece. So what is the

01:17:57.000 --> 01:17:59.000
takeaway from this? The takeaway is that when you're

01:17:59.000 --> 01:18:01.000
looking at attacking ideas, you

01:18:01.000 --> 01:18:03.000
should not forget that one of the most powerful

01:18:03.000 --> 01:18:05.000
techniques involves combining

01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:07.000
a mate threat with a threat

01:18:07.000 --> 01:18:09.000
on an undefended piece.

01:18:09.000 --> 01:18:11.000
Yet another type of instantiation

01:18:11.000 --> 01:18:13.000
is a scenario where

01:18:13.000 --> 01:18:15.000
I'm just getting a random position.

01:18:15.000 --> 01:18:17.000
A scenario like

01:18:17.000 --> 01:18:19.000
I don't know, like this one, right?

01:18:19.000 --> 01:18:21.000
Very often people forget that in such

01:18:21.000 --> 01:18:23.000
a situation, you can play Qh6

01:18:23.000 --> 01:18:25.000
and you can attack

01:18:25.000 --> 01:18:27.000
both the undefended rook and

01:18:27.000 --> 01:18:29.000
the h7 pawn. So there's a common

01:18:29.000 --> 01:18:31.000
attacking technique which

01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:33.000
essentially involves, in a

01:18:33.000 --> 01:18:35.000
situation like this, to deliver a

01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:37.000
check first, then to play

01:18:37.000 --> 01:18:39.000
Qh6 and fork the rook and the pawn.

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:41.000
Rather than going Qh6

