this move is correct because it doesn't involve any

01:29:01.000 --> 01:29:03.000
sacrifices. Yeah, it's e5.

01:29:03.000 --> 01:29:05.000
We literally just break through that

01:29:05.000 --> 01:29:07.000
deep freeze, and

01:29:07.000 --> 01:29:09.000
after fe, we play

01:29:09.000 --> 01:29:11.000
fg, and the pass

01:29:11.000 --> 01:29:13.000
pawns decide the game. And if

01:29:13.000 --> 01:29:15.000
gf here, we don't even need to sacrifice

01:29:15.000 --> 01:29:17.000
anything. We simply get this situation,

01:29:17.000 --> 01:29:19.000
and again, we have connected passers.

01:29:19.000 --> 01:29:21.000
They're just going to push on their own. Our rook

01:29:21.000 --> 01:29:23.000
is ideally placed.

01:29:23.000 --> 01:29:25.000
Could you start with e5 before f6?

01:29:25.000 --> 01:29:27.000
Yeah, you could.

01:29:27.000 --> 01:29:29.000
You could play e5 and then f6.

01:29:29.000 --> 01:29:31.000
The thing is, it is a waste of tempo

01:29:31.000 --> 01:29:33.000
though, because let's think about it mathematically.

01:29:33.000 --> 01:29:35.000
The good thing about starting with f6

01:29:35.000 --> 01:29:37.000
is that if white actually takes on f6,

01:29:37.000 --> 01:29:39.000
you've saved a tempo on e5,

01:29:39.000 --> 01:29:41.000
which you don't need to play. Now you can start

01:29:41.000 --> 01:29:43.000
pushing your h-pawn.

01:29:43.000 --> 01:29:45.000
By playing e5, this is a minor subtlety.

01:29:45.000 --> 01:29:47.000
You're giving white an extra tempo,

01:29:47.000 --> 01:29:49.000
which could become important because

01:29:49.000 --> 01:29:51.000
here, white is a little closer

01:29:51.000 --> 01:29:53.000
to creating a pass pawn of his own.

01:29:53.000 --> 01:29:55.000
There's no need to give white that tempo. You should only

01:29:55.000 --> 01:29:57.000
play e5 in response to f4.

01:29:57.000 --> 01:29:59.000
Does that make sense?

01:29:59.000 --> 01:30:01.000
In fact,

01:30:01.000 --> 01:30:03.000
inviting f4 is a good thing

01:30:03.000 --> 01:30:05.000
for black because now we have two

01:30:05.000 --> 01:30:07.000
pass pawns rather than one.

01:30:07.000 --> 01:30:09.000
Okay, so anyways,

01:30:09.000 --> 01:30:11.000
minor points. Our opponent decided

01:30:11.000 --> 01:30:13.000
to stay on the 5th rank. Now we play h6,

01:30:13.000 --> 01:30:15.000
which I think is a very good move.

01:30:15.000 --> 01:30:17.000
Oops, sorry.

01:30:17.000 --> 01:30:19.000
F4, Ke7, bringing our king up

01:30:19.000 --> 01:30:21.000
and preparing the eventual

01:30:21.000 --> 01:30:23.000
Rh8. And here I decided

01:30:23.000 --> 01:30:25.000
to take the opportunity to improve

01:30:25.000 --> 01:30:27.000
the placement of my rook.

01:30:27.000 --> 01:30:29.000
Okay, f4. Now, I'm not going to

01:30:29.000 --> 01:30:31.000
spend forever analyzing this with an engine.

01:30:31.000 --> 01:30:33.000
I think it's sort of a 50-50 game

01:30:33.000 --> 01:30:35.000
in that maybe white can draw this,

01:30:35.000 --> 01:30:37.000
but practically speaking, it would be very difficult.

01:30:37.000 --> 01:30:39.000
I will point out

01:30:39.000 --> 01:30:41.000
I think white went wrong.

01:30:41.000 --> 01:30:43.000
White's big mistake

01:30:43.000 --> 01:30:45.000
was probably in this position.

01:30:45.000 --> 01:30:47.000
I think white had a drawing idea.

01:30:47.000 --> 01:30:49.000
And I'm actually going to confirm

01:30:49.000 --> 01:30:51.000
this. Yes, I just confirmed this with the engine.

01:30:51.000 --> 01:30:53.000
I think this is a serious mistake.

01:30:53.000 --> 01:30:55.000
And here white had the same

01:30:55.000 --> 01:30:57.000
idea.

01:30:57.000 --> 01:30:59.000
Without this idea, white loses. It's a tactical

01:30:59.000 --> 01:31:01.000
idea that generates counterplay on the queen side.

01:31:01.000 --> 01:31:03.000
Very, very difficult. Let's see

01:31:03.000 --> 01:31:05.000
who can spot it. I will give you a hint. There's

01:31:05.000 --> 01:31:07.000
something that white can do

01:31:07.000 --> 01:31:09.000
on the queen side in order to

01:31:09.000 --> 01:31:11.000
favorably transform the pawn structure.

01:31:11.000 --> 01:31:13.000
What is it?

01:31:13.000 --> 01:31:15.000
Way too slow.

01:31:15.000 --> 01:31:17.000
a4, a5, a6. Way too slow. Black's pawn

01:31:17.000 --> 01:31:19.000
will be on h2 before you blink.

01:31:19.000 --> 01:31:21.000
Not c6.

01:31:21.000 --> 01:31:23.000
Too early.

01:31:23.000 --> 01:31:25.000
You have to start with rook a5,

01:31:25.000 --> 01:31:27.000
but that's the right direction.

01:31:27.000 --> 01:31:29.000
Why rook a5? What does this do?

01:31:29.000 --> 01:31:31.000
Well, this attacks the a-pawn.

01:31:31.000 --> 01:31:33.000
Black plays a6, and now we play

01:31:33.000 --> 01:31:35.000
c6, breaking apart the pawn

01:31:35.000 --> 01:31:37.000
chain. And in so doing,

01:31:37.000 --> 01:31:39.000
white creates this weakness

01:31:39.000 --> 01:31:41.000
that black now has to burn a tempo to

01:31:41.000 --> 01:31:43.000
defend, but most importantly,

01:31:43.000 --> 01:31:45.000
white creates a passed pawn of his own

01:31:45.000 --> 01:31:47.000
that can be used as a bargaining chip,

01:31:47.000 --> 01:31:49.000
making it more difficult and giving the

01:31:49.000 --> 01:31:51.000
rook more tasks.

01:31:51.000 --> 01:31:53.000
So just a good technique to have in your

01:31:53.000 --> 01:31:55.000
library of ideas, right? You attack

01:31:55.000 --> 01:31:57.000
a pawn, force it forward,

01:31:57.000 --> 01:31:59.000
and then you overload the other pawn.

01:31:59.000 --> 01:32:01.000
And I've seen this kind of thing before.

01:32:01.000 --> 01:32:03.000
Without this idea,

01:32:03.000 --> 01:32:05.000
white just does not have the defensive firepower

01:32:05.000 --> 01:32:07.000
to keep this pawn at bay.

01:32:07.000 --> 01:32:09.000
Maybe this is still a draw miraculously,

01:32:09.000 --> 01:32:11.000
but again, I think it's becoming harder and harder.

01:32:11.000 --> 01:32:13.000
And very importantly,

01:32:13.000 --> 01:32:15.000
we found the right opportunity

01:32:15.000 --> 01:32:17.000
to play e5. We're threatening to create

01:32:17.000 --> 01:32:19.000
a protected pass where we force the white

01:32:19.000 --> 01:32:21.000
to take, and already I think this is a winning endgame.

01:32:21.000 --> 01:32:23.000
King d5. I thought I

01:32:23.000 --> 01:32:25.000
played it very accurately. And this idea

01:32:25.000 --> 01:32:27.000
which I found was, I thought,

01:32:27.000 --> 01:32:29.000
very, very powerful, this rook on h5.

01:32:29.000 --> 01:32:31.000
Now you might say, well, how did

01:32:31.000 --> 01:32:33.000
you know that the rook belongs there?

01:32:33.000 --> 01:32:35.000
I actually had a game, if I remember

01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:37.000
correctly, where I

01:32:37.000 --> 01:32:39.000
had kind of a similar

01:32:39.000 --> 01:32:41.000
type of idea.

01:32:41.000 --> 01:32:43.000
Basically, the point is that

01:32:43.000 --> 01:32:45.000
you want to extract the most

01:32:45.000 --> 01:32:47.000
benefit from your rook.

01:32:47.000 --> 01:32:49.000
In general, in rook endgames,

01:32:49.000 --> 01:32:51.000
because you have so few pieces, you want

01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:53.000
your rook to be playing to its fullest potential.

01:32:53.000 --> 01:32:55.000
And there was a game long ago where I

01:32:55.000 --> 01:32:57.000
was able to save the game by

01:32:57.000 --> 01:32:59.000
kind of following that rule of using the rook

01:32:59.000 --> 01:33:01.000
to perform and fulfill

01:33:01.000 --> 01:33:03.000
as many tasks as possible. I was able

01:33:03.000 --> 01:33:05.000
to draw a GM in a game that I

01:33:05.000 --> 01:33:07.000
should have lost.

01:33:07.000 --> 01:33:09.000
So, this is the position that I had

01:33:09.000 --> 01:33:11.000
against that GM.

01:33:11.000 --> 01:33:13.000
Now,

01:33:13.000 --> 01:33:15.000
it's a very difficult position for white.

01:33:15.000 --> 01:33:17.000
But I was able to find

01:33:17.000 --> 01:33:19.000
a very interesting drawing technique.

01:33:19.000 --> 01:33:21.000
Basically, you ask

01:33:21.000 --> 01:33:23.000
yourself, what does black want?

01:33:23.000 --> 01:33:25.000
What black wants to do

01:33:25.000 --> 01:33:27.000
is to simply advance this king forward.

01:33:27.000 --> 01:33:29.000
I was able to find a

01:33:29.000 --> 01:33:31.000
unique fortress by playing e5.

01:33:31.000 --> 01:33:33.000
Two concepts are at

01:33:33.000 --> 01:33:35.000
play here. The first is, again, I would

01:33:35.000 --> 01:33:37.000
call this using the pawn as a bargaining chip

01:33:37.000 --> 01:33:39.000
where you might be afraid of something

01:33:39.000 --> 01:33:41.000
like rook b5, but you don't have to be because

01:33:41.000 --> 01:33:43.000
the moment black takes the e-pawn with the rook,

01:33:43.000 --> 01:33:45.000
we take the b-pawn and this is an obvious draw.

01:33:45.000 --> 01:33:47.000
Black, of course, plays

01:33:47.000 --> 01:33:49.000
king e6. Now the rook switches

01:33:49.000 --> 01:33:51.000
over to e8. And my opponent

01:33:51.000 --> 01:33:53.000
started to realize that he can't make progress

01:33:53.000 --> 01:33:55.000
because if he drives the king up,

01:33:55.000 --> 01:33:57.000
the rook drops down to e7,

01:33:57.000 --> 01:33:59.000
and you're able to win the f7 pawn

01:33:59.000 --> 01:34:01.000
and then quickly run the king over

01:34:01.000 --> 01:34:03.000
to the other side of the board.

01:34:03.000 --> 01:34:05.000
If white goes king d7,

01:34:05.000 --> 01:34:07.000
I threw in this move,

01:34:07.000 --> 01:34:09.000
and I go back to b8. And no matter

01:34:09.000 --> 01:34:11.000
what black does, anytime

01:34:11.000 --> 01:34:13.000
the king goes up to e6, the rook

01:34:13.000 --> 01:34:15.000
temporarily moves away from its ideal

01:34:15.000 --> 01:34:17.000
post to chase the king back down.

01:34:17.000 --> 01:34:19.000
And my

01:34:19.000 --> 01:34:21.000
opponent kept trying it, he

01:34:21.000 --> 01:34:23.000
failed, and then my rook was able to cut off

01:34:23.000 --> 01:34:25.000
the king, and eventually I was

01:34:25.000 --> 01:34:27.000
able to force white to give this pawn away

01:34:27.000 --> 01:34:29.000
and then get my king to the right spot

01:34:29.000 --> 01:34:31.000
in order to draw the game.

01:34:31.000 --> 01:34:33.000
Why was

01:34:33.000 --> 01:34:35.000
e5 important here?

01:34:35.000 --> 01:34:37.000
Let's say you try to play this without e5.

01:34:37.000 --> 01:34:39.000
Let's say you go king f1.

01:34:39.000 --> 01:34:41.000
Maybe this is not a great example.

01:34:41.000 --> 01:34:43.000
Oh, I was afraid of f6.

01:34:43.000 --> 01:34:45.000
I was afraid of this move.

01:34:45.000 --> 01:34:47.000
And I think what you're

01:34:47.000 --> 01:34:49.000
able to see here is that

01:34:49.000 --> 01:34:51.000
it's very hard for white's rook to do everything

01:34:51.000 --> 01:34:53.000
at once. If you go

01:34:53.000 --> 01:34:55.000
rook f8 and try to do the same thing,

01:34:55.000 --> 01:34:57.000
black's king basically squeezes through

01:34:57.000 --> 01:34:59.000
to a much more

01:34:59.000 --> 01:35:01.000
dangerous square. You're basically

01:35:01.000 --> 01:35:03.000
trying to keep the king boxed in. It's not

01:35:03.000 --> 01:35:05.000
an ideal example, but again, I'm just trying to

01:35:05.000 --> 01:35:07.000
help you understand the bigger picture,

01:35:07.000 --> 01:35:09.000
which is to try to maximize the potential

01:35:09.000 --> 01:35:11.000
of each piece, particularly the rook.

01:35:11.000 --> 01:35:13.000
And it doesn't

01:35:13.000 --> 01:35:15.000
always mean, you know,

01:35:15.000 --> 01:35:17.000
you shouldn't have a preconceived notion of what an

01:35:17.000 --> 01:35:19.000
active rook looks like.

01:35:19.000 --> 01:35:21.000
That's kind of the secondary

01:35:21.000 --> 01:35:23.000
concept here.

01:35:23.000 --> 01:35:25.000
Because you might look at this and say,

01:35:25.000 --> 01:35:27.000
this rook seems really passive because it's

01:35:27.000 --> 01:35:29.000
quote-unquote tied down

01:35:29.000 --> 01:35:31.000
to the protection of these pawns. Well, that's

01:35:31.000 --> 01:35:33.000
one way of looking at it, but it's also tying

01:35:33.000 --> 01:35:35.000
white down, right?

01:35:35.000 --> 01:35:37.000
Because it's tying down the king, it's

01:35:37.000 --> 01:35:39.000
keeping the king fully at bay.

01:35:39.000 --> 01:35:41.000
Notice how all of the infiltration squares are protected.

01:35:41.000 --> 01:35:43.000
And most importantly, it's keeping the rook

01:35:43.000 --> 01:35:45.000
at bay. Any time the rook moves, we push the pawn.

01:35:45.000 --> 01:35:47.000
Now, the last concept

01:35:47.000 --> 01:35:49.000
that I want to highlight

01:35:49.000 --> 01:35:51.000
is in this position

01:35:51.000 --> 01:35:53.000
where I missed that white

01:35:53.000 --> 01:35:55.000
was able to infiltrate from behind with

01:35:55.000 --> 01:35:57.000
king f6. And white

01:35:57.000 --> 01:35:59.000
could reach a very similar position

01:35:59.000 --> 01:36:01.000
to the one in the game,

01:36:01.000 --> 01:36:03.000
but I still think black is winning. And I think we

01:36:03.000 --> 01:36:05.000
win in exactly the same way that we won the game.

01:36:05.000 --> 01:36:07.000
We go b5, then we go

01:36:07.000 --> 01:36:09.000
king a4, and then we give up the h-pawn

01:36:09.000 --> 01:36:11.000
for the b-pawn, and we convert via the

01:36:11.000 --> 01:36:13.000
Lucina method. So we go here, here.

01:36:13.000 --> 01:36:15.000
We go here.

01:36:15.000 --> 01:36:17.000
We go rook c8 to cut

01:36:17.000 --> 01:36:19.000
off the king. And any time

01:36:19.000 --> 01:36:21.000
white takes the pawn, we take on a3.

01:36:21.000 --> 01:36:23.000
This is a theoretical win. This is a Lucina position.

01:36:23.000 --> 01:36:25.000
So the king is just

01:36:25.000 --> 01:36:27.000
unable to make it to the c-file.

01:36:27.000 --> 01:36:29.000
But I'll analyze this a little bit more

01:36:29.000 --> 01:36:31.000
on my own. This is getting a little bit too high level.

01:36:31.000 --> 01:36:33.000
For all intents and purposes, this is

01:36:33.000 --> 01:36:35.000
a winning endgame. This is almost impossible

01:36:35.000 --> 01:36:37.000
to save.

01:36:37.000 --> 01:36:39.000
But I've bored you enough

01:36:39.000 --> 01:36:41.000
with this endgame. Our opponent

01:36:41.000 --> 01:36:43.000
gave up a6. I took with the king.

01:36:43.000 --> 01:36:45.000
Notice what I did here.

01:36:45.000 --> 01:36:47.000
The reason I didn't take with a pawn is because

01:36:47.000 --> 01:36:49.000
having a corner pawn often

01:36:49.000 --> 01:36:51.000
referred to in a lot of rook endgames

01:36:51.000 --> 01:36:53.000
and pawn endgames. So even though it's counterintuitive

01:36:53.000 --> 01:36:55.000
to go backward, I thought the tempo

01:36:55.000 --> 01:36:57.000
was less essential than

01:36:57.000 --> 01:36:59.000
keeping that b-pawn.

01:36:59.000 --> 01:37:01.000
Which also immobilizes

01:37:01.000 --> 01:37:03.000
the a-pawn with the move

01:37:03.000 --> 01:37:05.000
b5.

01:37:05.000 --> 01:37:07.000
Okay.

01:37:07.000 --> 01:37:09.000
So

01:37:09.000 --> 01:37:11.000
in any case, we go b5.

01:37:11.000 --> 01:37:13.000
Now we give up one of the pawns

01:37:13.000 --> 01:37:15.000
strategically, but we

01:37:15.000 --> 01:37:17.000
cut off the king. And last

01:37:17.000 --> 01:37:19.000
thing, what if white had avoided

01:37:19.000 --> 01:37:21.000
taking the

01:37:21.000 --> 01:37:23.000
h4 pawn? Well, we could force

01:37:23.000 --> 01:37:25.000
white to take that pawn with a move like

01:37:25.000 --> 01:37:27.000
rook to g8, followed by rook

01:37:27.000 --> 01:37:29.000
to g3. Okay.

01:37:29.000 --> 01:37:31.000
So white is just not able to

01:37:31.000 --> 01:37:33.000
make it to the right area

01:37:33.000 --> 01:37:35.000
of the board at the right time.

01:37:35.000 --> 01:37:37.000
White's just not in time. We go b4, b3,

01:37:37.000 --> 01:37:39.000
b2. And notice how the rook actually

01:37:39.000 --> 01:37:41.000
cuts the king off along the third rank,

01:37:41.000 --> 01:37:43.000
so it's ideally placed on this square.

01:37:43.000 --> 01:37:45.000
If white plays

01:37:45.000 --> 01:37:47.000
rook takes h4, then you kind of know the drill.

01:37:47.000 --> 01:37:49.000
And the side checks are easily

01:37:49.000 --> 01:37:51.000
parried by bringing the king

01:37:51.000 --> 01:37:53.000
back to a4, and then blocking

01:37:53.000 --> 01:37:55.000
with a pawn. So the side checks are never like,

01:37:55.000 --> 01:37:57.000
it's never perpetual check here,

01:37:57.000 --> 01:37:59.000
because you basically use the same technique

01:37:59.000 --> 01:38:01.000
again and again. You go king a3,

01:38:01.000 --> 01:38:03.000
and then you go b3.

01:38:03.000 --> 01:38:05.000
And then king a2 and b2. You should remember that

01:38:05.000 --> 01:38:07.000
technique of dealing with side checks.

01:38:07.000 --> 01:38:09.000
And that's the basic

01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:11.000
premise for winning this game. Now the way

01:38:11.000 --> 01:38:13.000
our opponent played it, the only thing you have

01:38:13.000 --> 01:38:15.000
to know here is the technique of putting the king

01:38:15.000 --> 01:38:17.000
behind the pawn in order to advance the pawn

01:38:17.000 --> 01:38:19.000
one step further. Essentially pulling yourself

01:38:19.000 --> 01:38:21.000
up by the bootstraps. And then finally

01:38:21.000 --> 01:38:23.000
no Lucina needed, you just go rook a8,

01:38:23.000 --> 01:38:25.000
you choose the file

01:38:25.000 --> 01:38:27.000
that you will protect, and then you

01:38:27.000 --> 01:38:29.000
put the king on that file, and you promote

01:38:29.000 --> 01:38:31.000
the pawn. And that

01:38:31.000 --> 01:38:33.000
concludes this very, very

01:38:33.000 --> 01:38:35.000
complicated game. I basically only scratched the

01:38:35.000 --> 01:38:37.000
surface of the endgame, but we've been going

